        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Which Centr Treadmill Should You Buy?</h2>
<ul>
            <li><strong>Walking and easy jogging, folds away:</strong> the Centr Runr at $1,299, for its lifetime
            frame and motor warranty and tool-free setup.</li>
            <li><strong>You want streaming on the treadmill:</strong> the Runr-S, but only if you will use the
            screen; otherwise put the $400 towards a longer deck.</li>
            <li><strong>You run regularly or are tall:</strong> skip the Runr models. The Inspire T5 on the same
            store has a 60-inch deck, or compare our running picks.</li>
            <li><strong>You are training for HYROX:</strong> the Perform Tread if budget allows; the Tru Grit
            Runner if it does not.</li>
            <li><strong>You want a curved treadmill for running and sprints:</strong> the TrueForm Trainer or
            <a href="/assaultrunner-pro-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">AssaultRunner Pro</a> at about
            half the Perform Tread's price. Our <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best curved treadmills</a>
            roundup compares them, and <a href="/curved-treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill workouts</a>
            has HYROX-style sessions.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Before you choose between a curved and a motorized machine at all, read our
            <a href="/manual-vs-motorized-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual vs. motorized treadmill guide</a>:
            a curved belt is hard work for long steady runs, and the Perform Tread will not replace a normal
            treadmill for easy miles.
          </p>
